Its callers break down as 7 in production code and 31 in tests. The production callers are `AppendConditions` in `internal/filter/helpers.go`, `ListMemoRelations` in each of the three dialect packages (`store/db/postgres/memo_relation.go` has two calls, `store/db/sqlite/memo_relation.go` and `store/db/mysql/memo_relation.go` one each), `APIV1Service.validateFilter` in `server/router/api/v1/shortcut_service.go`, and `APIV1Service.validateAttachmentFilter` in `server/router/api/v1/attachment_service.go`. The test callers are spread across `internal/filter/functions_test.go` (17), `engine_test.go` (8), and `time_test.go` (6), each in a separate test function, which is what pushes the distinct-caller count so high.

One important caveat on the metric: if you count only non-test callers, the winner changes. `(*renderer).addArg` in `internal/filter/render.go:842` has 28 call sites from 14 distinct methods, all of them production code inside `render.go`, where it is the shared helper that appends a bound argument and returns its SQL placeholder. Next after that is `Schema.Field` (`internal/filter/schema.go:67`) with 19 production call sites in `internal/filter/parser.go` and `render.go`. So `internal/filter` dominates fan-in under `internal/` on any of these measures.

Two runner-ups worth naming, both counting tests: `filter.NewEngine` (`internal/filter/engine.go`) at ~32 and `util.GenUUID` (`internal/util/util.go`) at 28, though GenUUID is only 7 in production, with the other 21 concentrated in `server/router/api/v1/test/auth_test.go`, `authz_test.go`, and `server/router/fileserver/fileserver_test.go`.

A note on method and confidence: there is no Go toolchain or Python in this environment, so these counts come from pattern matching rather than a real call graph, which means they are approximate. I did vet the results for the usual false positives: raw matching initially ranked `SortVersion.Len` (`internal/version/version.go`) at 317 and `Schedule.Next` (`internal/scheduler/parser.go`) at 59, but those resolve to `require.Len`/`assert.Len` from testify and `rows.Next` from `database/sql` respectively. `SortVersion.Len` in fact has no explicit callers, since it is invoked implicitly through `sort.Sort`. Similarly, an early count of 41 for `scheduler.New` was almost entirely `errors.New`. Because interface dispatch and same-name methods can't be fully resolved this way, treat the figures as ballpark; the ordering among the top few is solid, but the exact numbers could shift by a small amount.
